open_questions:
  - "Is 'Chaney' Annie's authentic birth surname, or an alias, married name, or recording error?"
  - "Who were Annie's parents? The France vs. Ohio birthplace discrepancy for her father remains the highest-priority unresolved conflict."
  - "What were the actual circumstances of Annie's divorce from John Farney Woodruff? This is under active research and will substantially expand this page."
  - "What became of David Henry Woodruff? Born 13 June 1882 per the family Bible and named as a minor child in John and Annie's divorce pleadings (September 1899), David Henry is entirely absent from the family Deaths register and was unknown to the family until uncovered by this research. The Victor Record (22 Sept 1900) reports a Henry Woodruff, described as a prospector living in a cabin six miles west of Victor, removed to St. Nicholas Hospital with a severe attack of typhoid fever — consistent with a boy who was a minor in his parents' custody dispute a year earlier now living independently at 17 or 18, as was common for young men on the mining frontier. A Colorado Springs Gazette item, 'To Investigate a Death' (8 Dec 1900), reports the death — at 'the Sisters' hospital,' almost certainly the same Catholic-run St. Nicholas Hospital — of a 17-year-old 'Henry Woodruff' whose parents lived at Divide, Colorado, following a relapse of the same illness; an autopsy was ordered after the attending physician stated the fever itself was not the cause of death. The Gazette's stated age (17) is a year younger than David Henry's documented birth date would make him at this date — ordinary period reporting imprecision, or a sign this is a different Henry Woodruff entirely. Working Hypothesis, not yet confirmed by name. Notably, Divide Cemetery is confirmed as an established Woodruff family burial ground — it holds William Wilson Woodruff (his body returned from Wyoming specifically for burial there), John's fourth wife Ida B. Williams (Emmons), and a headstone marking an intended, ultimately unused, burial plot for John himself beside Ida. David Henry does not appear among Divide Cemetery's Woodruff burials, which sharpens rather than resolves the mystery: he may not have been buried there at all, may have been buried without a marker and never recorded on Find a Grave, or the 1900 Henry Woodruff of Victor/Divide may not be him after all."
  - "Was there a legal marriage to Henry Fry, or did Annie adopt the name and the identity of his widow without one? This question is connected to disputed claims made during the Woodruff divorce proceedings and is under active research."
  - "Who was the 'infirm uncle' Annie reportedly married before John Farney Woodruff? No record, name, or date has been located; this comes from family account only."
  - "Where did Annie live, and with whom, in the more than three decades between her 1899 divorce and her return to family in 1932? Trinidad and Walsenburg are documented; the full picture is not."
  - "Who was 'Mary Fry Phelps'? Lee Roy's 1953 death certificate gives his mother's maiden name as 'Mary Fry Phelps,' Missouri — matching neither 'Annie E. Chaney' (her name throughout her marriage to John) nor any other documented identity, though it notably echoes the 'Fry' surname she is independently documented using from about 1910 onward. Possible explanations include: 'Fry' was a name she carried from before her marriage to John, not merely one she adopted afterward; 'Phelps' represents a distinct, still-earlier identity; or the informant — quite possibly hospital staff working from Lee Roy's own WWI service record rather than a family member's memory — introduced an error decades removed from any firsthand knowledge. Unresolved."
  - "Florence Woodruff's 1954 divorce from Sherry O. Curtis states their date of separation as about one year before filing — but the couple's documented 1920 Colorado marriage to Edwin L. Bennett implies they had, in practice, been apart for roughly 34 years by then. The stated separation date appears to be a legal formality rather than a factual account, likely intended to regularize the earlier (unlawful) 1920 Bennett marriage without a public accounting of the intervening decades."
  - "No divorce decree has been located for Edwin L. Bennett's first marriage, to Lena Garrison (10 Jan 1916, Creede, Colorado). Ted's own sworn 1954 marriage application states he was divorced in Colorado in 1920, but no independent decree has yet been found to confirm it."
  - question: "A commonly repeated misattribution names Annie as \"Anna DeMaro,\" daughter of Francis and America DeMaro of Ironton, Ohio."
