<p>Annie E. Chaney's early life remains the project's most stubborn brick wall. Her parentage, the authenticity of "Chaney" as a birth surname, and her origins before 1877 are all unresolved, and a death certificate's claim that her father was born in France sits unreconciled against her own 1880 census entry giving his birthplace as Ohio.</p>

<p>What can be said with confidence begins in Indiana, where her first known child, Edna Latitia, was born on 28 August 1877, about six weeks before John Farney Woodruff's own divorce pleading states the couple married, on 10 October 1876. No marriage license has been located. From Indiana the family moved to Missouri, where Annie, John, Edna, and a second daughter were enumerated together on 3 June 1880 in Lone Oak Township, Bates County, the only surviving census to capture this family as a household, since the 1890 census did not survive and the marriage ended in divorce in 1899. That second daughter, recorded by the enumerator simply as "Maud," eleven months old, is Anna Maud Woodruff, Shannon's great-grandmother; the age recorded independently corroborates her proven birth date of 2 July 1879. Five more children followed, Robert Franklin, David Henry, and William Wilson in Missouri, Florence in Kansas, and Lee Roy and Warren Browning in Colorado, before the marriage ended in divorce in Teller County on 14 September 1899. The full circumstances of that divorce are still being researched and will substantially expand this page.</p>

<p>What happened to Annie afterward is only beginning to come into focus. Family memory holds that she left Colorado after the divorce, and although she later returned to live in towns across the state, she did not live with her own family again until the very end of her life. By 1910 or 1911, a Trinidad city directory records her as "Anna Fry, widow of Henry," the first dated appearance of a name she would use for the rest of her life, though no marriage license for this union has been found, and whether it represents a genuine marriage is an open question connected to claims made during her own divorce proceedings. A postcard her daughter Anna Maud sent her in December 1913 is addressed to "Mrs. Annie Fry," and asks, with evident sadness, why her mother doesn't write. A 1923 newspaper item records her visiting Anna Maud in Creede. Her 1932 death certificate gives her name as Mrs. Anna E. Fry.</p>

<p>The final chapter, as related by Anna Maud's granddaughter, Anna Maud "Polly" Davis, is the most affecting part of Annie's story. After decades of separation, Anna Maud prepared a cabin for her mother at the family ranch, and the family looked forward to a long-delayed reunion. Annie was reportedly afraid to come, saying she would die there; the family reassured her, and she came. Within days of her arrival, Anna Maud found her mother dead in her bed, a detail the 1932 death certificate confirms independently, recording the cause of death as a probable cerebral hemorrhage and noting she was "found dead in bed." Mother and daughter were reunited only to be parted again almost immediately after.</p>

<p><strong>A note on what's not yet here:</strong> this page deliberately holds back the detailed circumstances of the 1899 divorce, including allegations made by both parties, John's subsequent remarriage, and the death of Annie's son David Henry in the months that followed, pending a dedicated research session to review the full case file and contemporary newspaper coverage with the care those documents deserve.</p>